 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for delivery.** Committed changes are pushed to browsers over WebSocket connections managed on the project's own infrastructure. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected because it introduces several seconds of lag and generates repeated reads at scale; a hosted realtime service was rejected to avoid recurring per-message fees and provider coupling. Self-hosted WebSockets deliver subsecond fan-out at the cost of owning connection capacity and reconnect logic. See [`docs/decisions/delivery-mechanism.md`](decisions/delivery-mechanism.md).
